const productCases={"maria":{"name":"MARIA APARTMENTS","industry":"Hospitality / Short-Term Accommodation","platform":"Bilingual website · Booking platform · Administrative dashboard · Telegram automation","location":"Ternopil, Ukraine","role":"Independent Digital Product Designer & Developer","scope":"Business analysis · Product strategy · Brand identity · UX/UI · Development · Integrations · Testing · Deployment","strapline":"From manual apartment rentals to an integrated hospitality management ecosystem","links":[{"label":"MARIA APARTMENTS","href":"https://www.bymariaapartments.com"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","<p>Maria Apartments is a short-term accommodation business operating a portfolio of 19 apartments across several locations in Ternopil, Ukraine.</p><p>The project began with an established rental business that had customers, properties and operational experience, but lacked a unified brand identity and an integrated digital infrastructure.</p><p>Reservations were managed through Excel spreadsheets. Guests relied on direct communication to check availability, confirm bookings and arrange their arrival. Payments involved bank transfers and cash, while key handovers required physical coordination. Instagram served as an important customer-facing channel, but there was no dedicated platform connecting apartment discovery, reservations, payments and internal operations.</p><p>My responsibility was to transform this fragmented experience into a coherent digital ecosystem.</p><p>I independently managed the project across creative direction, user experience, technical implementation and operational integration. The work included establishing a new brand, producing visual content, designing and developing a bilingual accommodation website, implementing a booking and payment system, creating an administrative management platform and developing a custom Telegram bot for guest assistance and contactless check-in.</p><p>The result was not simply a redesigned website.</p><p>It was a connected hospitality management system designed to support the relationship between guests, administrators, reservations, payments, physical properties and daily business operations.</p>"],["Understanding the Business","<h3>The original operating model</h3><p>Before the transformation, the business operated under the generic description &quot;Daily Apartment Rentals&quot; rather than a recognizable independent brand.</p><p>Individual apartments were presented primarily through existing listings and social media. Customers frequently needed to contact an administrator to obtain information that could otherwise have been available through a self-service platform.</p><p>The reservation process depended on several disconnected interactions.</p><p>A potential guest would inquire about an apartment, ask about available dates, receive pricing information and coordinate payment directly with the business.</p><p>A security deposit was typically transferred to a bank card, while the remaining accommodation cost was paid in cash upon arrival.</p><p>After booking, the administrator recorded reservation details in Excel, coordinated arrival arrangements, managed physical keys and later processed the deposit return.</p><p>Other activities, including cleaning schedules, property maintenance and financial tracking, required additional manual coordination.</p><p>These processes were functional individually, but their separation created unnecessary dependencies.</p><h3>The core business problems</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Fragmented information*</em></p><p>Reservation records, apartment availability, payments, guest communication and operational tasks were managed through separate channels.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Administrative dependency*</em></p><p>Routine guest questions and booking actions required direct staff involvement.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited self-service*</em></p><p>Customers could not complete the entire accommodation journey independently through an official website.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Payment friction*</em></p><p>Financial transactions were divided between different payment methods and stages of the stay.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Operational complexity*</em></p><p>Managing 19 properties required ongoing coordination of arrivals, departures, cleaning, maintenance and availability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Inconsistent brand presentation*</em></p><p>The absence of a unified identity made the business appear as a collection of rental listings rather than a recognizable accommodation provider.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited reporting*</em></p><p>Without integrated analytics, evaluating occupancy, reservation activity and property-level performance required manual work.</p><p>The challenge was to address these problems as parts of one system rather than introduce isolated solutions.</p>"],["Discovery & UX Research","<p>My research approach was strongly influenced by direct participation in the business's daily operations.</p><p>Rather than relying exclusively on a design brief, I gained firsthand insight into guest communication, booking coordination, arrival procedures and administrative responsibilities.</p><p>This allowed me to examine the complete service experience, including interactions that happened outside a conventional website.</p><p>I analyzed how guests obtained information, how availability was confirmed, how payments were coordinated and what administrators needed to prepare an apartment for the next arrival.</p><p>I also examined the relationships between reservations, cleaning schedules, security deposits and physical access.</p><p>This revealed an important distinction between the needs of different users.</p><h3>Guest needs</h3><p>Guests needed to:</p><ul><li>Find suitable accommodation without extensive messaging.</li><li>Compare apartments by location, price, capacity and amenities.</li><li>Understand availability and accommodation costs.</li><li>Complete a reservation with clear payment instructions.</li><li>Receive reliable confirmation.</li><li>Access practical arrival and accommodation information.</li><li>Obtain assistance without repeating their booking details.</li></ul><h3>Administrator needs</h3><p>Administrators needed to:</p><ul><li>View reservations across all 19 apartments.</li><li>Identify occupied and available dates.</li><li>Create and modify bookings.</li><li>Coordinate cleaning between departures and arrivals.</li><li>Track deposits and reservation payments.</li><li>Manage apartment information and operational issues.</li><li>Prepare guests for check-in.</li><li>Control physical keybox access.</li><li>Access structured business reporting.</li></ul><h3>Research conclusion</h3><p>The principal challenge was not a lack of individual digital tools.</p><p>It was the absence of a shared system connecting customer-facing experiences with operational data.</p><p>This conclusion shaped the architecture of the entire project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>Research methodology note: The discovery process was based on operational observation, firsthand experience and workflow analysis rather than a formal controlled user study.</em></p>"],["Product Strategy","<p>I defined the transformation around a connected set of digital and physical touchpoints.</p><p>The strategy had three major objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*01. Establish a recognizable hospitality brand*</em></p><p>Create a consistent identity capable of representing all 19 apartments across digital channels and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*02. Build an independent customer booking journey*</em></p><p>Allow guests to discover accommodation, check dates, understand pricing, book and pay without requiring continuous administrator assistance.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*03. Centralize internal operations*</em></p><p>Provide administrators with a dedicated management environment connected to reservations, payment records, apartment availability, cleaning and guest communication.</p><p>A fourth consideration influenced the entire project: the connection between software and physical accommodation.</p><p>A successful online reservation still needed to result in a guest arriving at the correct property, receiving authorized access and finding an apartment prepared for occupancy.</p><p>This made service design and operational reliability just as important as visual interface design.</p>"],["Brand Identity & Creative Direction","<h3>From a generic rental service to Maria Apartments</h3><p>The transformation began with a new brand identity.</p><p>I developed the name <strong>Maria Apartments</strong>, replacing the original generic positioning with an identifiable hospitality brand.</p><p>The new identity needed to communicate comfort, reliability and professional management without feeling excessively corporate or disconnected from the nature of the accommodation service.</p><p>I designed the logo, established the visual direction and created a consistent system of typography, color and supporting visual elements.</p><p>A warm, light visual approach was selected to make the brand feel welcoming while maintaining clarity across functional interfaces.</p><p>The identity was designed to work in several environments: property listings, booking forms, Instagram content, printed materials and physical guest touchpoints.</p><h3>Photography &amp; property presentation</h3><p>Accommodation is a visually driven service.</p><p>Guests rely heavily on photographs to assess the condition, atmosphere and suitability of a property.</p><p>I photographed apartment interiors, selected and retouched images, and prepared visual assets for consistent presentation across the website and social media.</p><p>The objective was not to make different properties appear identical.</p><p>It was to establish a consistent standard of visual quality while preserving the individual characteristics of each apartment.</p><h3>Physical brand materials</h3><p>I also designed branded business cards and keychains, prepared them for production and coordinated ordering.</p><p>These materials extended the new identity beyond screens.</p><p>The keychains were particularly relevant because physical access was an essential part of the accommodation experience.</p><p>Connecting digital presentation with physical objects helped establish continuity throughout the guest journey.</p>"],["Instagram Rebranding & Digital Presence","<p>Instagram was already an important discovery and communication channel for the business.</p><p>However, the existing account lacked the coherence of a unified brand.</p><p>I redesigned the profile presentation, developed a consistent content direction and established a visual system aligned with the new Maria Apartments identity.</p><p>The work included updated profile information, visual content, post design, publication and organized navigation through Instagram Highlights.</p><p>I also connected the social media presence to the official website.</p><p>This reflected a deliberate strategic decision.</p><p>Instagram would remain a channel for discovery, brand communication and customer engagement, while the website would become the primary environment for property exploration, reservations and payments.</p><p>The objective was to reduce dependence on manual social media conversations as the main booking infrastructure.</p>"],["Website UX/UI Design","<h3>A website designed around the complete booking journey</h3><p>The official Maria Apartments website was developed as a responsive, bilingual platform supporting all 19 apartments.</p><p>Its primary purpose was to transform accommodation discovery into a structured and actionable experience.</p><p>The information architecture was organized around the guest's practical decision-making process:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Discover → Compare → Evaluate → Check Availability → Book → Pay → Receive Confirmation*</em></p><p>Each stage needed to provide enough information for the user to proceed confidently.</p><h3>Interactive building facade</h3><p>One of the distinctive visual features of the website is its interactive building facade.</p><p>Instead of functioning as a purely decorative homepage illustration, the building provides an alternative way to explore available accommodation.</p><p>The interactive windows connect the visual presentation to apartment discovery.</p><p>This approach gave the business a memorable digital identity while making the interface itself part of the experience.</p><p>However, visual originality was not allowed to replace usability.</p><p>The interactive building was supported by more conventional browsing tools, ensuring that customers could still explore properties through familiar catalog and filtering patterns.</p><p>The design decision balanced brand differentiation with practical navigation.</p><h3>Apartment catalog &amp; filtering</h3><p>The website includes a structured catalog of 19 apartments, allowing guests to explore different properties within a consistent interface.</p><p>Filtering supports practical accommodation decisions, including available dates, guest capacity, location and pricing considerations.</p><p>Because properties are distributed across different areas of Ternopil, location-based navigation was an important requirement.</p><p>The catalog was designed to help visitors narrow their options before committing to a detailed property review.</p><h3>Individual apartment pages</h3><p>Each apartment needed enough information to support an informed booking decision.</p><p>I organized property pages around consistent content categories, including:</p><ul><li>Apartment photography.</li><li>Descriptions and accommodation characteristics.</li><li>Guest capacity.</li><li>Relevant amenities.</li><li>Location information.</li><li>Pricing.</li><li>Availability.</li><li>Booking actions.</li></ul><p>Consistent structure was particularly important for a portfolio containing many different properties.</p><p>It allowed visitors to compare options without repeatedly learning a new page layout.</p><h3>Responsive design &amp; localization</h3><p>The website supports Ukrainian and English.</p><p>Both language versions were integrated into the customer-facing experience to make accommodation information accessible to local and international visitors.</p><p>The responsive design accounts for different screen sizes, including mobile browsing, where property discovery, date selection and booking actions require additional attention to hierarchy and touch interaction.</p>"],["Booking Experience & Payment Integration","<h3>Replacing a fragmented reservation process</h3><p>The original booking journey required multiple conversations, manual availability checks and separate payment arrangements.</p><p>I designed a connected booking experience that brought these actions into a structured digital flow.</p><p>The intended customer journey became:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Apartment Selection → Date Selection → Guest Details → Price Review → Online Payment → Booking Confirmation*</em></p><h3>Availability &amp; reservation validation</h3><p>Accommodation availability cannot be treated as a static piece of information.</p><p>A property may become unavailable between the moment a guest views the calendar and the moment they attempt to complete a reservation.</p><p>For this reason, availability is validated through server-side reservation logic.</p><p>The system includes safeguards against conflicting reservations and overlapping booking dates.</p><h3>Pricing logic</h3><p>The booking system was designed to account for accommodation-specific pricing conditions.</p><p>The checkout calculation can incorporate selected dates, length of stay, guest-related charges and security deposit requirements.</p><p>The aim was to present the relevant booking cost within the reservation process instead of relying on a separate conversation to establish the amount.</p><h3>Full online payment</h3><p>I integrated <strong>WayForPay</strong> to support online payment for the accommodation and security deposit.</p><p>Moving from a combination of bank transfers and cash to an integrated checkout experience was one of the project's most significant operational changes.</p><p>However, introducing digital payment also required careful handling of transaction states.</p><p>The implementation included signed payment requests, server-side verification, webhook processing, transaction status handling and reconciliation mechanisms.</p><p>A successful browser redirect alone was not treated as sufficient evidence of payment completion.</p><p>The system needed to verify the payment independently.</p><h3>Handling failure and uncertainty</h3><p>The reservation process also needed to account for situations where a payment was pending, failed, delayed or reported more than once.</p><p>These conditions informed the design of payment states and reservation safeguards.</p><p>The objective was to avoid both customer confusion and inconsistent booking records.</p><p>This work demonstrated that transactional UX involves more than a checkout interface.</p><p>It requires the visible customer journey and underlying system behavior to remain consistent under real-world conditions.</p>"],["Administrative Dashboard","<h3>Replacing Excel with an operational management system</h3><p>A major component of the project was the development of a dedicated administrative dashboard.</p><p>The original Excel-based workflow stored reservation information but did not provide a connected environment for coordinating the broader accommodation operation.</p><p>I designed and developed an interface organized around the tasks administrators actually needed to perform.</p><p>The dashboard consolidated information and actions that had previously been distributed across spreadsheets, messages and manual processes.</p><h3>Reservation management</h3><p>The system supports administrative reservation workflows, including:</p><ul><li>Reviewing existing reservations.</li><li>Creating bookings manually.</li><li>Modifying reservation details.</li><li>Extending stays.</li><li>Transferring reservations between apartments.</li><li>Managing reservation statuses.</li><li>Accessing guest and booking information.</li></ul><p>Manual booking creation was an important requirement because not every reservation originated through the public website.</p><p>The platform needed to accommodate existing business channels without forcing every customer through the same digital entry point.</p><h3>Visual reservation calendar</h3><p>I implemented a visual administrative calendar to provide a clearer view of apartment occupancy and upcoming bookings.</p><p>For a business managing 19 properties, seeing the relationships between apartments, dates and reservation statuses is more useful than examining isolated spreadsheet rows.</p><p>The calendar supports day-to-day planning and helps administrators understand the operational consequences of booking changes.</p><h3>Cleaning coordination</h3><p>Cleaning schedules are directly connected to check-outs and upcoming arrivals.</p><p>The administrative system introduced structured cleaning records and scheduling information based on reservation activity.</p><p>This helped connect apartment availability with the physical preparation required between guest stays.</p><p>Importantly, the software supports planning and coordination; the actual cleaning remains a human operational responsibility.</p><h3>Maintenance &amp; operational tasks</h3><p>The dashboard includes functionality for tracking maintenance issues and other property-related tasks.</p><p>This provides a more organized alternative to relying entirely on informal messages and separate records.</p><h3>Security deposits</h3><p>Deposit tracking was integrated into the administrative environment.</p><p>The system provides records and visibility around deposit-related responsibilities, while physical or financial actions that require administrator involvement remain controlled operational processes.</p><h3>Administrative UX principles</h3><p>The dashboard was designed around information clarity, operational context and task efficiency.</p><p>The goal was not to reproduce Excel inside a website.</p><p>It was to give administrators a purpose-built environment where reservation data could support meaningful actions.</p>"],["Business Analytics & Reporting","<p>Before the transformation, the business had limited centralized tools for examining operational performance.</p><p>Information relevant to occupancy, reservation value and individual properties existed, but evaluating it required manual calculation or reconciliation.</p><p>I introduced an analytics and reporting layer within the administrative platform.</p><p>The system organizes business information around reservation activity, occupancy, financial records and property-level performance.</p><p>This allows administrators to examine historical and upcoming activity through structured reporting rather than disconnected records.</p><p>The design also distinguishes operational information needed for immediate decisions from broader reporting used for planning.</p><p>The project established the infrastructure for better visibility into the business.</p><p>It did not, however, establish verified long-term improvements in revenue or occupancy attributable to the new platform.</p>"],["Telegram Bot & Guest Experience Automation","<h3>Designing the experience beyond checkout</h3><p>Completing a reservation is not the end of an accommodation journey.</p><p>Guests still need practical information about arrival, access, apartment rules and their stay.</p><p>Previously, much of this information was provided through direct communication with an administrator.</p><p>I developed a custom Telegram bot to support the guest experience after booking.</p><p>The bot was built with Python and aiogram and connected to the reservation ecosystem.</p><h3>Personalized booking access</h3><p>An early version of the concept involved reservation-specific MA codes.</p><p>During development, this approach evolved into personalized Telegram links.</p><p>Rather than remembering and manually entering a code, guests could open a reservation-linked Telegram experience.</p><p>The flow includes phone verification and account binding, allowing booking information to be associated with an authorized Telegram account.</p><p>This reduced friction in the intended guest journey while supporting controlled access to reservation details.</p><h3>Guest assistance</h3><p>The bot provides structured access to information relevant to the guest's stay.</p><p>This includes:</p><ul><li>Reservation information.</li><li>Pre-arrival guidance.</li><li>Check-in instructions.</li><li>Apartment-specific information.</li><li>FAQ navigation.</li><li>Help resources.</li><li>Administrator contact options.</li></ul><p>The interaction structure was designed around practical guest questions rather than an unnecessarily complex conversational interface.</p><h3>Contactless check-in</h3><p>A central feature was the connection between verified reservations and physical keybox access.</p><p>The system includes operational configuration for six physical keyboxes associated with the apartment portfolio.</p><p>Authorized guests can receive relevant access instructions according to their reservation and the applicable access conditions.</p><p>This required particular attention to the boundary between public information and restricted data.</p><p>An apartment description can be available to anyone.</p><p>Its physical access code cannot.</p><p>The bot therefore separates general guest assistance from information requiring current authorization.</p>"],["Security & Physical Access Control","<p>Because the platform connects digital reservations to physical accommodation, access security was an important design and engineering consideration.</p><p>Several safeguards were implemented across different stages of the guest journey.</p><h3>Reservation verification</h3><p>Restricted instructions are linked to verified booking information.</p><p>The bot checks relevant authorization conditions instead of relying solely on information recorded during a guest's first interaction.</p><p>This matters when a booking is cancelled, modified, completed or no longer authorized for access.</p><h3>Controlled keybox code activation</h3><p>One of the more important operational decisions concerned the relationship between digital records and physical keyboxes.</p><p>Updating a code in software does not automatically change the code installed on a physical lockbox.</p><p>The administrative workflow therefore separates entering a replacement code from confirming its physical activation.</p><p>A replacement code is not made available to guests until the administrator confirms that the physical keybox has been updated.</p><p>This prevents the system from distributing access information that may not yet work in the real world.</p><h3>Conflicting administrative changes</h3><p>The keybox management system also accounts for potential conflicts between administrative actions.</p><p>Version checks help prevent an outdated administrative view from overwriting more recent code information.</p><h3>Booking and payment integrity</h3><p>The reservation system incorporates server-side safeguards, including payment verification, duplicate-processing protection and database-level measures against overlapping bookings.</p><p>These safeguards are not primarily visual design features.</p><p>They are essential parts of making the customer-facing experience dependable.</p>"],["Technical Architecture & Development","<p>My work extended beyond product strategy and interface design into the implementation and integration of the underlying digital infrastructure.</p><p>The platform was developed as several connected components with distinct responsibilities.</p><h3>Public website</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Next.js · React · TypeScript*</em></p><p>The customer-facing web application supports apartment discovery, responsive interfaces, availability information and booking interactions.</p><h3>Database</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Neon PostgreSQL*</em></p><p>The database supports structured reservation data, apartment information and the relationships required by booking and administrative workflows.</p><h3>Payment system</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*WayForPay*</em></p><p>The integration handles checkout requests, payment verification, status updates and transaction processing safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative platform</h3><p>The management interface connects reservation data with operational workflows, including property availability, cleaning schedules, guest records, financial tracking and reporting.</p><h3>Telegram automation</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Python · aiogram*</em></p><p>The separately developed bot provides authenticated guest assistance and controlled access to booking-related information.</p><h3>Hosting &amp; infrastructure</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Vercel · Render · SQLite · GitHub*</em></p><p>The website was deployed through Vercel.</p><p>The Telegram bot was migrated from Railway to Render, with persistent storage used for its operational state.</p><p>GitHub supported source control and the development workflow.</p><h3>My development responsibility</h3><p>I independently directed and implemented the connected system using modern development tools and AI-assisted workflows.</p><p>My responsibilities included translating business requirements into technical structures, designing interfaces, developing application functionality, coordinating integrations, evaluating system behavior, debugging and deployment.</p><p>The project required continuity between design decisions and engineering implementation.</p><p>A booking interaction could affect the database.</p><p>A payment confirmation could affect reservation status.</p><p>A change to a reservation could affect Telegram access permissions.</p><p>Understanding these dependencies was essential to delivering the project as a coherent product.</p>"],["Iteration & Problem-Solving","<p>The project was developed iteratively rather than through a completely fixed sequence of design and development phases.</p><p>Several important decisions changed as operational requirements became clearer.</p><h3>Iteration 01: MA codes to personalized links</h3><p><strong>Initial approach:</strong> Guests would access reservation information using manually entered booking codes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> This introduced an additional step and required guests to retain and enter a code.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Personalized Telegram links connected reservations to a verification and account-binding flow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Reduce unnecessary interaction while preserving controlled access to booking information.</p><h3>Iteration 02: Payment confirmation and reservation reliability</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Payment completion involved multiple systems and potentially asynchronous responses.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Browser redirects and payment notifications could not be treated as perfectly synchronized or independently authoritative.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Server-side verification, reconciliation and duplicate-processing safeguards became part of the transaction workflow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Protect reservation and financial records against delayed or repeated payment events.</p><h3>Iteration 03: Database-level booking protection</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Availability displayed in the interface could change while a customer was completing a reservation.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Interface validation alone could not reliably prevent conflicting bookings.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Additional server-side and database-level safeguards were incorporated.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Maintain reservation integrity even when multiple actions occur close together.</p><h3>Iteration 04: Physical keybox activation</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Digital code updates and physical lockbox changes happened through different processes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> A stored code could be distributed before the physical keybox was updated.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Separate code entry from administrator-confirmed physical activation.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Keep software permissions consistent with the real-world state of the property.</p><h3>Iteration 05: Infrastructure refinement</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> The Telegram service required reliable hosting and persistence of operational data.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> The bot was migrated from Railway to Render with attention to preserving stored information.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Support the continuity of a separately hosted service connected to the wider booking ecosystem.</p><p>These iterations illustrate an important aspect of the project: decisions were evaluated against operational requirements, not only against the appearance of individual interfaces.</p>"],["Before & After","<p>The transformation changed how the business presented itself and how its core processes were organized.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Business Area</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity</td><td>Generic apartment rental positioning</td><td>Maria Apartments brand identity</td></tr><tr><td>Digital presence</td><td>Instagram and external listings</td><td>Branded social presence and official website</td></tr><tr><td>Apartment discovery</td><td>Direct inquiries and separate listings</td><td>Structured catalog of 19 apartments</td></tr><tr><td>Availability</td><td>Manual checks and spreadsheets</td><td>Integrated availability calendars</td></tr><tr><td>Booking</td><td>Administrator-coordinated process</td><td>Structured online reservation flow</td></tr><tr><td>Payments</td><td>Bank transfer and cash</td><td>Integrated WayForPay checkout</td></tr><tr><td>Administration</td><td>Excel-based management</td><td>Dedicated management dashboard</td></tr><tr><td>Cleaning</td><td>Manual scheduling</td><td>Reservation-connected cleaning records</td></tr><tr><td>Guest communication</td><td>Direct messaging and calls</td><td>Personalized Telegram assistance</td></tr><tr><td>Check-in</td><td>Physical coordination and key handover</td><td>Controlled contactless access workflow</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Manual calculations</td><td>Centralized operational analytics</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The value of these changes lies in their connection.</p><p>A redesigned website alone would not have resolved spreadsheet-based administration.</p><p>A booking form alone would not have solved payment verification.</p><p>A Telegram bot alone would not have provided reliable authorization.</p><p>The project required these elements to function as parts of one service.</p>"],["Results & Delivered Outcomes","<p>The project produced a complete set of interconnected creative, technical and operational deliverables.</p><h3>Brand transformation</h3><p>A new name, logo, visual identity, photography direction, social media presentation and printed brand materials established a consistent identity for the accommodation business.</p><h3>Customer-facing platform</h3><p>The business gained an official bilingual website presenting all 19 apartments through structured listings, individual property information, availability and booking functionality.</p><h3>Digital reservations &amp; payment</h3><p>A connected booking experience introduced online checkout, pricing calculations, payment-status handling and server-side reservation safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative management</h3><p>A centralized dashboard introduced structured reservation management, visual calendars, guest records, cleaning coordination, deposit tracking, maintenance records and business reporting.</p><h3>Automated guest assistance</h3><p>The Telegram system provided personalized booking access, verification, structured information and controlled check-in instructions.</p><h3>Connected infrastructure</h3><p>The website, database, payment gateway, administrative system and Telegram bot were integrated into a shared digital operating environment.</p><h3>Validation &amp; measurement</h3><p>The project documentation includes automated tests and deployment checks supporting the technical implementation.</p><p>However, the available evidence does not establish a controlled long-term comparison of revenue, conversion rates, customer satisfaction or operational cost savings.</p><p>Some real-world scenarios, including comprehensive production payment validation and physical check-in success rates, require further documented evaluation.</p><p>For this reason, I distinguish between delivered functionality, expected operational benefits and independently measured business outcomes.</p><p>The most defensible result is the transformation of previously fragmented workflows into a connected digital system.</p>"],["My Individual Contribution","<p>Maria Apartments was a multidisciplinary project in which I worked across responsibilities commonly divided between several specialist roles.</p><p>My involvement began with understanding the business and continued through creative direction, interface design, technical development and deployment.</p><p>I was responsible for translating operational problems into product requirements, determining the experience across customer and administrative interfaces, implementing connected functionality and refining decisions as technical and business constraints emerged.</p><p>The project combined several professional disciplines:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Business &amp; Product Strategy*</em></p><p>Understanding the rental operation, defining requirements and organizing the relationship between digital tools and business processes.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX/UI &amp; Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Designing discovery, booking, payment, administration and guest assistance experiences.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand Identity &amp; Creative Direction*</em></p><p>Establishing a recognizable identity and applying it consistently across digital and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Photography &amp; Content Production*</em></p><p>Creating and preparing property photography, branded visuals and social media materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Web &amp; Application Development*</em></p><p>Implementing responsive interfaces, server-side functionality, database connections and administrative features.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Integrations &amp; Automation*</em></p><p>Connecting payment processing, reservation records, Telegram assistance and physical access workflows.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Testing &amp; Deployment*</em></p><p>Validating functionality, addressing integration challenges and managing production-oriented technical delivery.</p><p>This breadth of responsibility required an understanding of the entire service rather than a narrow focus on one design deliverable.</p>"],["Reflection","<p>Maria Apartments fundamentally expanded the scope of what began as a branding and digital presence project.</p><p>The most important challenge was not creating a visually attractive website.</p><p>It was developing a reliable relationship between customer interactions, administrative processes, financial transactions, stored data and physical accommodation.</p><p>Working directly with the business revealed how easily a small operational detail can affect the wider user experience.</p><p>A confusing payment state can create uncertainty for a guest.</p><p>An overlapping reservation can create a serious administrative problem.</p><p>An incorrectly activated keybox code can prevent someone from entering an apartment.</p><p>A disconnected cleaning schedule can affect whether accommodation is ready for the next arrival.</p><p>These are not isolated interface problems.</p><p>They are service problems that require coordinated product, design and engineering decisions.</p><p>The experience reinforced the importance of understanding a business before selecting technologies or designing screens.</p><p>It also demonstrated the value of maintaining responsibility across the complete development process.</p><p>Maria Apartments represents my ability to work independently across brand identity, product strategy, UX/UI design, software development, technical integrations and operational automation.</p><p>The final result is more than a website.</p><p>It is a connected digital foundation for an accommodation business, built around the practical needs of guests, administrators and the service itself.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*A complete hospitality experience, designed from the first impression to the apartment door.*</em></p>"]]},"arced":{"name":"ARCED CONSTRUCTION GROUP","industry":"Construction / Concrete Services / Tile Installation","platform":"Two commercial websites · Digital presence","location":"Winnipeg, Manitoba, Canada","role":"Independent UX/UI Designer & Web Developer","scope":"Business analysis · UX strategy · Responsive UX/UI · Development · Local SEO · Testing · Deployment","strapline":"Two independent websites. One connected digital identity.","links":[{"label":"ARCED Construction","href":"https://arcedconstruction.ca"},{"label":"ARCED Tile","href":"https://arcedtile.ca"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","<p>ARCED Construction Group is a Canadian construction business operating in Winnipeg, Manitoba, with services covering concrete work and professional tile installation.</p><p>The project involved designing and developing two distinct commercial websites representing separate service directions under one business identity.</p><p>Although concrete construction and tile installation belong to the broader construction industry, they involve different customer expectations, search behavior, project requirements and decision-making processes.</p><p>A homeowner looking for concrete driveway installation does not necessarily need the same information as someone planning a bathroom renovation or kitchen backsplash.</p><p>Attempting to present both services through one undifferentiated website experience would make the business harder to navigate and reduce the clarity of its individual service offerings.</p><p>I approached ARCED as a connected digital presence rather than a single website project.</p><p>The objective was to create two independent, service-focused online experiences that could maintain a recognizable shared identity while communicating different specializations.</p><p>I independently managed the work from requirements gathering and website architecture through UX/UI design, responsive development and production delivery.</p><p>The project included developing service-oriented content structures, preparing existing brand assets for digital use, establishing conversion pathways, designing responsive interfaces, integrating customer inquiry functionality and organizing content around local search intent.</p><p>The result was a pair of commercial websites designed to help potential customers understand ARCED's services, evaluate the company and take the next step toward requesting an estimate.</p>"],["Business Context & Initial Challenge","<p>Construction websites serve a different purpose from purely editorial or experimental digital experiences.</p><p>Potential customers rarely visit a contractor's website simply to explore its visual identity.</p><p>They usually arrive with a practical problem.</p><p>They need a driveway installed, a concrete surface replaced, a bathroom tiled or a renovation completed.</p><p>Their primary concerns often include the contractor's capabilities, service area, reliability, project requirements, pricing expectations and how to initiate contact.</p><p>For ARCED, the challenge was to translate specialist construction services into an accessible digital experience.</p><p>The business needed to communicate two distinct directions:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Concrete Construction*</em></p><p>Work involving concrete installations, foundations, driveways, patios, sidewalks, garage floors and related concrete projects.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Work involving bathroom and kitchen tiling, floors, walls, showers, backsplashes, waterproofing, tile replacement and finishing details.</p><p>The digital strategy needed to distinguish these service categories without fragmenting the business identity.</p><h3>Core project challenges</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Service differentiation*</em></p><p>Present concrete and tile services clearly enough that visitors can immediately understand which website is relevant to their needs.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Credibility*</em></p><p>Establish a professional online presence appropriate for a local construction business.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Information clarity*</em></p><p>Explain technical services without requiring visitors to understand specialist terminology.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Lead generation*</em></p><p>Create straightforward pathways from service discovery to an estimate request.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local relevance*</em></p><p>Organize content for customers searching for construction services in Winnipeg.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand consistency*</em></p><p>Maintain a relationship between the two websites while allowing each to develop its own service-specific structure.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Responsive usability*</em></p><p>Support visitors browsing from mobile devices, tablets and desktop computers.</p><p>The project required a balance between business communication, visual design, user experience and technical implementation.</p>"],["Discovery & Requirements Analysis","<p>I began by understanding the company's service structure, target customers and practical requirements.</p><p>The discovery process included direct communication about the business's operations, the services it offered, its intended audience, its geographical market and the information customers needed before requesting work.</p><p>I also reviewed the Canadian construction market and relevant competitor approaches to organizing services, presenting expertise and encouraging inquiries.</p><p>This work informed both the information architecture and the content hierarchy.</p><h3>Business requirements</h3><p>The websites needed to communicate:</p><ul><li>Which construction services ARCED provides.</li><li>The difference between concrete and tile specializations.</li><li>Where the company operates.</li><li>What customers can expect during a project.</li><li>Which factors affect pricing.</li><li>How to request an estimate.</li><li>How to contact the business.</li><li>What information can help the company assess a potential job.</li></ul><p>The business serves residential and commercial customer needs, which also influenced the presentation of different service types.</p><h3>Customer requirements</h3><p>From the visitor's perspective, the experience needed to answer several immediate questions.</p><p>What does this company do?</p><p>Does it provide the service I need?</p><p>Does it operate in my area?</p><p>Can I understand the process before contacting anyone?</p><p>What affects the price?</p><p>How can I request an estimate?</p><p>These questions became the foundation of the UX strategy.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>The project involved requirements gathering and competitive analysis. It did not include a documented formal usability study or controlled user interviews.</em></p>"],["UX Strategy","<h3>Designing around customer intent</h3><p>The most important UX decision was to organize the websites around what potential customers were trying to accomplish.</p><p>Instead of placing the brand story ahead of everything else, the architecture prioritizes service clarity.</p><p>A visitor should be able to recognize the relevant service category quickly, understand its practical applications and locate a clear inquiry action.</p><p>The strategy was based on four principles.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*01. Immediate Relevance*</em></p><p>Communicate the service specialization and location early in the page.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*02. Clear Evaluation*</em></p><p>Give visitors enough information to assess whether ARCED is suitable for their project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*03. Reduced Contact Friction*</em></p><p>Make estimate requests and contact information accessible without unnecessary navigation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*04. Consistent Business Identity*</em></p><p>Maintain visual and structural relationships between the two sites without making them interchangeable.</p><p>This approach ensured that the design remained commercially purposeful rather than relying exclusively on visual presentation.</p>"],["Why Two Separate Websites?","<p>The decision to develop two individual websites was central to the project.</p><p>Although both service directions belong to ARCED, their audiences often begin with different problems.</p><p>Someone considering a concrete driveway may be evaluating the size of an outdoor area, surface preparation, site conditions and the nature of the installation.</p><p>Someone considering tile work may be comparing finishes, waterproofing requirements, room types, surface preparation and installation details.</p><p>These differences affect what information should appear first, how the services should be grouped and which project details deserve emphasis.</p><h3>One brand, two customer journeys</h3><p>I established a shared approach to visual identity, typography, interface consistency and contact presentation.</p><p>At the same time, I developed separate service architectures tailored to the two specializations.</p><p>This allowed:</p><ul><li>More specific service descriptions.</li><li>Clearer customer journeys.</li><li>Independent local search positioning.</li><li>More relevant project presentation.</li><li>Distinct information hierarchies.</li><li>Service-specific inquiry pathways.</li></ul><p>The objective was not to create two unrelated brands.</p><p>It was to give each specialization enough independence to communicate effectively while maintaining the connection to ARCED Construction Group.</p>"],["ARCED Construction — Concrete Services Website","<p><strong>Website:</strong> https://arcedconstruction.ca</p><h3>Product objective</h3><p>The construction website was designed to present ARCED's concrete services to residential and commercial customers in the Winnipeg market.</p><p>Its primary responsibility was to communicate the company's capabilities and provide a straightforward path toward a project inquiry.</p><p>Concrete construction involves practical decisions that may depend on location, surface type, dimensions, preparation requirements and the intended use of the finished structure.</p><p>The information architecture therefore needed to focus on recognizable service categories rather than abstract descriptions of construction expertise.</p><h3>Service-oriented architecture</h3><p>I structured the content around concrete-related work, with service categories covering areas such as:</p><ul><li>Concrete driveways.</li><li>Foundations.</li><li>Patios.</li><li>Sidewalks and walkways.</li><li>Garage floors.</li><li>Concrete steps.</li><li>Concrete slabs.</li><li>Concrete replacement and preparation work.</li></ul><p>These categories helped translate a broad construction specialization into specific services customers could recognize.</p><h3>User experience considerations</h3><p>The design needed to support visitors who might have limited technical knowledge.</p><p>Rather than requiring users to understand construction methods before making an inquiry, the website presents the company's work through customer-relevant applications.</p><p>The experience emphasizes:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Service clarity*</em></p><p>Visitors should be able to identify whether the company handles their type of project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Project context*</em></p><p>Construction imagery and project-oriented content help communicate the nature of the work.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Process transparency*</em></p><p>Explaining the steps involved in arranging construction work can reduce uncertainty before an inquiry.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Contact accessibility*</em></p><p>Estimate requests and contact actions need to remain easy to locate.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local relevance*</em></p><p>Content should connect the services to the Winnipeg market.</p><h3>Conversion strategy</h3><p>The website is structured to guide visitors from understanding a service toward initiating contact.</p><p>The primary journey is:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Discover a Service → Understand the Scope → Evaluate the Company → Request an Estimate*</em></p><p>This approach turns service information into a practical path toward a business inquiry.</p><p>The objective was to create an accessible commercial website, not a complex customer portal that would introduce unnecessary steps.</p>"],["ARCED Tile — Specialist Installation Website","<p><strong>Website:</strong> https://arcedtile.ca</p><h3>Product objective</h3><p>ARCED Tile focuses on professional tile installation for residential and commercial interiors.</p><p>Its website needed to explain a more visually detailed service category while supporting users at different stages of renovation planning.</p><p>Tile installation customers may already know the room they want to renovate, but not the precise service terminology.</p><p>Someone looking to improve a bathroom may need information about floor tiles, shower walls, waterproofing or existing tile removal.</p><p>The information architecture needed to accommodate both general customer intentions and specific installation requirements.</p><h3>Service architecture</h3><p>I organized the interface around recognizable categories, including:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Bathroom Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Presenting bathroom-related installation work and finishing considerations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Kitchen Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Covering kitchen surfaces and installations that need to accommodate cabinetry, counters and daily use.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Backsplashes*</em></p><p>Highlighting decorative and practical tile installation for kitchens and other interiors.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Floor Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Communicating flooring applications involving different tile types and surface conditions.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Wall Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Presenting installation work for walls and decorative surfaces.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Shower Tile &amp; Waterproofing*</em></p><p>Explaining the relationship between installation quality, preparation and water protection.</p><p>Additional content addresses tile removal, replacement, repairs and different material requirements.</p><h3>Information hierarchy</h3><p>The website guides visitors from broad service recognition toward greater detail.</p><p>The primary page structure includes:</p><ul><li>Clear service positioning.</li><li>Service categories.</li><li>Supporting explanations.</li><li>Reasons to consider the company.</li><li>Installation process.</li><li>Project presentation.</li><li>Pricing guidance.</li><li>Frequently asked questions.</li><li>Estimate request options.</li><li>Contact information.</li></ul><p>This sequence reflects the different stages of a potential customer's decision.</p><p>A visitor who already understands the service can proceed toward an estimate.</p><p>A visitor who needs more information can explore the process, project examples and practical considerations before contacting the company.</p>"],["Designing the Customer Journey","<p>Both websites were designed around a common commercial objective: helping relevant visitors become informed prospective customers.</p><p>However, I did not approach conversion as simply placing more buttons on a page.</p><p>A successful inquiry experience requires users to understand what they are requesting.</p><h3>Stage 01. Recognition</h3><p>The visitor identifies the relevant service and confirms that the business operates in the appropriate location.</p><h3>Stage 02. Exploration</h3><p>The visitor reviews service information and understands the broad scope of the work.</p><h3>Stage 03. Evaluation</h3><p>Supporting content helps clarify the installation process, project considerations and company presentation.</p><h3>Stage 04. Inquiry</h3><p>The user is given a clear opportunity to request an estimate or contact the company.</p><h3>Stage 05. Business Follow-Up</h3><p>The website supports the transition from digital inquiry to direct communication, where project-specific requirements can be assessed.</p><p>The interfaces were designed to support this progression without forcing every visitor through an identical sequence.</p><p>Clear navigation and repeated contextual contact opportunities make the experience more flexible.</p>"],["Lead Generation & Estimate Requests","<p>For a construction business, the website's commercial value is closely connected to the quality and clarity of customer inquiries.</p><p>The design therefore needed to support users who were ready to discuss a project as well as visitors who still required more information.</p><p>I incorporated enquiry-oriented structures and contact functionality into the websites.</p><h3>Clear calls to action</h3><p>Estimate requests are presented in relation to the services being explored.</p><p>This allows a visitor to act when they have enough context rather than requiring them to return to the top of the page or search for contact details.</p><h3>Practical information collection</h3><p>A useful construction inquiry generally benefits from information about the project type, location, dimensions, requirements and contact details.</p><p>The website's estimate-oriented forms are designed to support this kind of initial communication.</p><h3>Balancing information and friction</h3><p>A form that asks too little may produce an inquiry lacking the details required for a useful response.</p><p>A form that asks too much may discourage a potential customer from completing it.</p><p>The objective was to maintain an understandable entry point to the estimate process while leaving detailed project assessment to direct follow-up.</p><p>The websites do not attempt to replace the contractor's professional evaluation.</p><p>They create a structured starting point for that conversation.</p>"],["Pricing Guidance & Cost Estimation","<p>One of the recurring challenges in construction services is pricing uncertainty.</p><p>Customers may want to understand the approximate cost of a project before contacting a contractor.</p><p>However, installation prices depend on multiple variables, including materials, project size, preparation, complexity and site conditions.</p><p>ARCED Tile includes a cost-estimation entry point as part of its customer experience.</p><h3>UX objective</h3><p>The purpose of preliminary pricing guidance is to help visitors understand the factors that influence a quote.</p><p>It is not to present an automated estimate as a guaranteed final contract price.</p><h3>Design considerations</h3><p>The experience needs to communicate:</p><ul><li>The type of work being considered.</li><li>Relevant project dimensions or scope.</li><li>Material and installation considerations.</li><li>Preparation requirements.</li><li>Why final pricing may require further assessment.</li></ul><p>By making pricing factors more understandable, the website can help visitors approach a conversation with more realistic expectations.</p><p>This supports the broader goal of transparent communication while preserving the role of an accurate project-specific estimate.</p>"],["Trust, Credibility & Service Communication","<p>Construction customers frequently evaluate a business before contacting it.</p><p>Unlike a low-cost online purchase, a renovation or concrete installation may involve significant expense, disruption and reliance on a contractor's professional workmanship.</p><p>For this reason, credibility was an important part of the content strategy.</p><h3>Communicating capabilities</h3><p>Service explanations and project-oriented imagery give visitors a clearer understanding of the work the company offers.</p><h3>Explaining the process</h3><p>Process content helps make the transition from initial inquiry to completed installation more understandable.</p><h3>Addressing uncertainty</h3><p>Frequently asked questions provide space to clarify common concerns about materials, service areas, installation requirements and estimates.</p><h3>Presenting business commitments</h3><p>The Tile website communicates company-stated information about insurance, a two-year workmanship warranty and the availability of residential and commercial services.</p><p>These details are presented as business information rather than independent certifications or guarantees of project outcomes.</p><h3>Avoiding artificial social proof</h3><p>Credibility should come from verifiable business information, actual services, relevant project materials and clear communication.</p><p>The case study does not claim a measured increase in trust or rely on invented customer testimonials.</p><p>The design objective was to give prospective customers enough relevant information to assess the business for themselves.</p>"],["Local SEO & Content Architecture","<p>ARCED operates in a geographically defined service market.</p><p>Its websites therefore needed to communicate not only what the company does, but also where those services are available.</p><p>I incorporated local search considerations into the content planning and page architecture.</p><h3>Service-specific search intent</h3><p>Concrete services and tile installation generate different search intentions.</p><p>The content strategy distinguished terms associated with the two specializations rather than treating all construction-related searches as interchangeable.</p><p>Examples of relevant search categories include:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Concrete*</em></p><p>Concrete contractor, concrete driveways, patios, foundations, sidewalks and related installation services in Winnipeg.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Tile*</em></p><p>Tile installation, bathroom tile, kitchen backsplash, floor tile, shower tile and related services in Winnipeg.</p><h3>Content organization</h3><p>The websites use clear service positioning and descriptive content to support visitors searching for particular types of work.</p><p>The planning included attention to:</p><ul><li>Service-specific headings.</li><li>Descriptive page titles.</li><li>Local market relevance.</li><li>Logical content hierarchy.</li><li>Internal navigation.</li><li>Relevant service explanations.</li><li>Contact and estimate entry points.</li><li>Responsive presentation.</li></ul><h3>UX and SEO alignment</h3><p>The objective was not to fill pages with repeated keywords.</p><p>It was to make important services understandable to both visitors and search engines.</p><p>A well-structured service page should answer a recognizable customer question while clearly communicating its subject.</p><p>This approach supports search-oriented content without sacrificing readability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*The project established an SEO-oriented website structure. It does not claim verified Google rankings, organic traffic growth or search performance improvements without supporting analytics.*</em></p>"],["Visual Identity & Art Direction","<p>The two websites needed a visual language appropriate for a professional construction business.</p><p>The design direction prioritized clarity, confidence and consistency.</p><p>The objective was to communicate a dependable commercial identity without making the interfaces visually generic.</p><h3>Shared brand foundation</h3><p>ARCED Construction and ARCED Tile were designed to remain recognizable as parts of one business.</p><p>This required consistent treatment of:</p><ul><li>Brand assets.</li><li>Typography.</li><li>Layout principles.</li><li>Navigation patterns.</li><li>Calls to action.</li><li>Contact information.</li><li>Interface components.</li></ul><h3>Brand asset preparation</h3><p>I prepared and vectorized existing brand materials for more consistent digital use.</p><p>This included working with the existing ARCED identity rather than claiming authorship of the original brand concept.</p><p>The goal was to ensure that the available assets could be used clearly and consistently across different screen sizes and interface contexts.</p><h3>Service-specific presentation</h3><p>While maintaining a shared identity, the two websites allow their subject matter to influence the visual presentation.</p><p>Concrete services benefit from clear construction-oriented content and practical project information.</p><p>Tile installation benefits from detailed visual examples, material presentation and attention to finishes.</p><p>The design system needed enough flexibility to accommodate both without losing consistency.</p>"],["Responsive UX/UI Design","<p>The websites were designed for desktop, tablet and mobile experiences.</p><p>Responsive design was an important requirement because construction customers may access a contractor's website in different contexts.</p><p>Someone might browse services from a computer while planning a renovation.</p><p>Another visitor might open the website on a phone while comparing contractors or preparing to request an estimate.</p><p>The interface needed to remain understandable across those situations.</p><h3>Responsive navigation</h3><p>Navigation structures were adapted to preserve access to important content on smaller screens.</p><h3>Service presentation</h3><p>Service descriptions and supporting imagery were organized to maintain clear hierarchy without requiring desktop-sized layouts.</p><h3>Contact accessibility</h3><p>Inquiry and contact actions needed to remain accessible on mobile devices.</p><h3>Content readability</h3><p>Typography, spacing and component proportions were designed to support scanning and comprehension.</p><h3>Interaction consistency</h3><p>Interactive elements needed to behave predictably across different devices.</p><p>The objective was to preserve the same essential customer journey rather than simply shrink the desktop interface.</p>"],["Content Design & UX Writing","<p>A technically accurate service description is not automatically a useful one.</p><p>Construction terminology can be unfamiliar to customers who are planning their first renovation or concrete project.</p><p>I therefore treated content structure as part of the user experience.</p><h3>Clear service naming</h3><p>Service categories were organized around recognizable customer needs.</p><h3>Practical explanations</h3><p>Supporting descriptions communicate what the work involves and where it may be relevant.</p><h3>Action-oriented language</h3><p>Calls to action were designed to make the next step understandable.</p><h3>Frequently asked questions</h3><p>FAQ content provides additional detail without requiring every visitor to read long explanations before identifying the service they need.</p><h3>Consistent tone</h3><p>The business presentation was developed to sound professional, direct and approachable.</p><p>This helped connect the brand's visual identity to its written communication.</p><p>Content design was an important part of the project because the websites needed to function as commercial communication tools, not merely visual portfolios.</p>"],["Front-End Development & Technical Delivery","<p>My involvement continued beyond visual design into the implementation of the production websites.</p><p>I independently managed the transition from the planned information architecture and interface direction to responsive, functioning web experiences.</p><h3>Development scope</h3><p>The work included:</p><ul><li>Implementing page structures.</li><li>Developing responsive layouts.</li><li>Creating reusable interface patterns.</li><li>Integrating visual assets.</li><li>Building interactive components.</li><li>Implementing navigation behavior.</li><li>Connecting contact and inquiry functionality.</li><li>Preparing customer-facing content.</li><li>Supporting basic technical SEO requirements.</li><li>Testing different viewport sizes.</li><li>Preparing and delivering the published websites.</li></ul><h3>Two independent digital properties</h3><p>The main technical and organizational consideration was maintaining separate websites with their own content and navigation while preserving a consistent business identity.</p><p>Each site needed to function independently for its relevant audience.</p><p>At the same time, both needed to feel connected through the ARCED brand.</p><h3>Independent project ownership</h3><p>I was responsible for coordinating the work across requirements, design decisions, implementation and final delivery.</p><p>This meant evaluating the websites as complete products rather than treating design and development as unrelated stages.</p><p>Decisions about navigation affected content organization.</p><p>Decisions about service categories affected search-oriented structure.</p><p>Decisions about estimate requests affected interface hierarchy.</p><p>Maintaining responsibility across those relationships helped preserve consistency between the original objectives and the implemented websites.</p>"],["Key Design Decisions","<p>Several decisions were particularly important to the project's direction.</p><h3>Decision 01. Separate the two specializations</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Concrete construction and tile installation involve different customer needs and search intentions.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Develop two independent service-focused websites under a connected brand identity.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Give each specialization enough space to communicate clearly without creating unnecessary competition between unrelated service categories.</p><h3>Decision 02. Prioritize services over generic brand messaging</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Visitors looking for a contractor usually want to confirm practical service relevance quickly.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Place clear service positioning and recognizable service categories prominently within the experience.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Help users establish whether the company offers the work they need before asking them to explore secondary content.</p><h3>Decision 03. Connect information with inquiry actions</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Visitors may decide to contact the company at different points in their evaluation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Integrate estimate and contact opportunities into the relevant service journeys.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Make the transition from understanding a service to requesting information more direct.</p><h3>Decision 04. Use a consistent but flexible visual system</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Two independent websites can easily feel unrelated or become visually repetitive.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Retain shared brand patterns while adapting content and presentation to each specialization.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Preserve business recognition without sacrificing the clarity of individual services.</p><h3>Decision 05. Make local relevance explicit</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Construction services are geographically constrained, and customers need to identify appropriate providers.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Structure content around relevant services and the Winnipeg market.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Connect customer search intent with clear service and location information.</p><h3>Decision 06. Explain pricing without promising false precision</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Construction costs depend on conditions that may not be fully known during an initial website visit.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Support preliminary pricing understanding while maintaining a clear route toward a project-specific estimate.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Improve transparency without presenting an automated calculation as a binding professional quote.</p>"],["Project Challenges & Problem-Solving","<h3>Maintaining distinction without fragmentation</h3><p>The first challenge was making the concrete and tile websites feel independent enough to serve different audiences while retaining a shared business identity.</p><p>The solution was to separate their content architecture while maintaining common visual and interaction principles.</p><h3>Balancing technical information and accessibility</h3><p>Construction services require specific explanations, but overly technical content can create unnecessary friction.</p><p>The interface therefore needed to communicate practical service applications before expanding into specialized details.</p><h3>Designing for commercial intent</h3><p>The websites were not intended as purely artistic digital experiences.</p><p>Visual choices needed to support comprehension, credibility and clear inquiry pathways.</p><p>This required evaluating design decisions against the practical goals of the business.</p><h3>Supporting different devices</h3><p>Responsive implementation required adapting information density, navigation and interaction patterns without losing access to important content.</p><h3>Coordinating design and development</h3><p>Managing the websites independently meant ensuring that the implemented result reflected the intended UX, rather than treating development as a separate final step.</p><p>These challenges reinforced the importance of approaching commercial web projects as connected systems of content, design and functionality.</p>"],["Before & After","<p>The project established a dedicated digital presence for two service directions within ARCED Construction Group.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Before the Project</th><th>Delivered Direction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Digital presence</td><td>No established dedicated website</td><td>Two published commercial websites</td></tr><tr><td>Service positioning</td><td>Services needed clear online separation</td><td>Distinct concrete and tile experiences</td></tr><tr><td>Information architecture</td><td>No dedicated digital service structure</td><td>Organized, service-oriented navigation</td></tr><tr><td>Visual consistency</td><td>Existing brand materials requiring digital preparation</td><td>Coordinated visual presentation</td></tr><tr><td>Customer inquiry</td><td>Direct contact without a structured website journey</td><td>Dedicated inquiry and estimate pathways</td></tr><tr><td>Local discovery</td><td>No dedicated website architecture</td><td>Winnipeg-oriented service content</td></tr><tr><td>Device support</td><td>No existing responsive website experience</td><td>Responsive desktop, tablet and mobile interfaces</td></tr><tr><td>Service information</td><td>No centralized online presentation</td><td>Structured explanations and supporting content</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This comparison describes the delivered digital capabilities rather than a measured increase in sales or customer acquisition.</p>"],["Results & Deliverables","<p>The project resulted in two independently published commercial websites.</p><h3>ARCED Construction</h3><p>A dedicated online experience presenting the concrete construction specialization through service-oriented information architecture, responsive design and customer inquiry pathways.</p><h3>ARCED Tile</h3><p>A specialist tile installation website combining structured service content, project presentation, process explanations, pricing guidance, frequently asked questions and estimate request functionality.</p><h3>Shared brand presentation</h3><p>The sites maintain a recognizable relationship through consistent brand assets, visual principles and interface patterns.</p><h3>Digital customer journeys</h3><p>Both websites provide a structured route from service discovery to contacting the business.</p><h3>Local content foundation</h3><p>The content architecture supports clear communication of services within the Winnipeg market.</p><h3>Responsive implementation</h3><p>The interfaces were implemented for use across different screen sizes and interaction contexts.</p><h3>Production delivery</h3><p>Both websites were developed and published as accessible commercial properties.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Project outcomes should be evaluated accurately.*</em></p><p>The confirmed deliverables are two websites, their content and interface systems, their inquiry pathways and their production implementation.</p><p>The available project information does not establish verified changes in conversion rates, lead volume, search rankings or revenue.</p><p>These would require post-launch analytics and a defined measurement period.</p>"],["My Individual Contribution","<p>ARCED demonstrates my ability to independently manage commercial website projects across strategy, design and implementation.</p><p>My work included understanding business requirements, researching the relevant market, defining the information architecture, establishing visual direction, preparing brand assets and developing the production websites.</p><p>Rather than working only on isolated interface screens, I was responsible for shaping the complete digital experience around the business's services and customer needs.</p><p>The project required competency in several connected areas.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Business &amp; Requirements Analysis*</em></p><p>Understanding the company's service structure, customer groups, geographic market and commercial objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX Strategy*</em></p><p>Designing separate customer journeys for two distinct service specializations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Organizing service content, navigation and inquiry pathways.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX/UI Design*</em></p><p>Developing responsive interfaces that balance visual quality with practical usability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand Adaptation*</em></p><p>Preparing existing identity assets for a consistent digital presentation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX Writing &amp; Content Strategy*</em></p><p>Presenting construction services through clear, customer-oriented explanations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local SEO Planning*</em></p><p>Structuring content around relevant service categories and geographic search intent.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Front-End Development*</em></p><p>Implementing responsive layouts, interactive components and functional web experiences.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Testing &amp; Delivery*</em></p><p>Preparing the websites for publication and maintaining responsibility for the final implementation.</p><p>The defining aspect of my contribution was continuity across these responsibilities.</p><p>I was not simply producing visual layouts for someone else to interpret.</p><p>I managed the design and development process as a complete commercial deliverable.</p>"],["Reflection","<p>ARCED was an important exercise in translating a traditional service business into a purposeful digital experience.</p><p>The project demonstrated that effective commercial web design begins with understanding how customers make decisions.</p><p>A construction website does not need to overwhelm visitors with visual effects.</p><p>It needs to communicate relevant services, establish a professional presentation, explain important details and make the next action clear.</p><p>The most significant strategic decision was treating concrete construction and tile installation as two different customer journeys rather than forcing them into one generic interface.</p><p>This required both consistency and restraint.</p><p>The business identity needed to remain recognizable, but the content architecture had to respect the differences between the two specializations.</p><p>The project also reinforced the importance of treating content, UX, visual design and technical implementation as connected disciplines.</p><p>The value of the websites came from how those elements worked together.</p><p>My individual responsibility extended from the initial business requirements to the published digital products.</p><p>ARCED represents my ability to independently design, develop and deliver commercial websites for an international client, adapting the experience to different services, customer intentions and business objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Two specialized websites, one recognizable business identity, and a digital experience built around the way real customers search for construction services.*</em></p>"]],"images":["assets/commercial-covers/arced-construction.png","assets/commercial-covers/arced-tile.png"]},"vanta":{"name":"Monday Balance","industry":"Financial Technology","platform":"iOS / Android","location":"New York, USA","role":"UX/UI Designer · Product Designer","scope":"Product research · Information architecture · Interaction design · UI design · Prototyping","strapline":"Making personal finance understandable","sections":[["Project Overview","Monday Balance is a personal finance management application designed to help people understand spending habits, organise budgets and make more informed financial decisions. The product focuses on the gap between recording transactions and understanding what those transactions mean.","The work covered product research, UX strategy, information architecture, interaction design, UI design and prototyping."],["Context & Problem Definition","Many finance applications expose users to complex dashboards, excessive categorisation and accounting terminology. Users could see individual transactions but struggled to recognise broader patterns, connect everyday spending with long-term goals or maintain a budgeting system that demanded constant effort."],["Users & Their Needs","The product considered young professionals with regular income, freelancers managing irregular income and goal-oriented savers. Their practical questions were simple: Can I afford this? Where did my money go? What progress am I making? Those questions shaped the hierarchy more than financial terminology did."],["Research & Discovery","Discovery focused on personal financial routines and on the moments when people actually need information. The key design insight was cognitive effort: the product needed to answer an immediate question first, then offer deeper analysis when the user chose to explore it."],["UX Strategy & Information Architecture","The application was organised around five destinations: Overview, Activity, Budgets, Goals and Insights. Overview became the primary interaction point, showing current balance, monthly expenditure, remaining budget and one relevant insight instead of every available metric."],["Key Design Decisions","Progressive disclosure moved detailed analytics into secondary screens. Visual budget tracking replaced dense tables with labelled progress indicators. Flexible income management supported both fixed salaries and irregular income. Quick-add transactions, suggested categories and reusable templates reduced repetitive input friction."],["UI & Interaction Design","Dark surfaces, restrained accents, large type and generous spacing created a calmer alternative to trading-platform interfaces. Important values received clear typographic priority. Empty states, incomplete transactions, warnings and non-colour status cues were considered as part of the core experience."],["Iteration & Design Evolution","The first dashboard placed too many analytical cards in competition for attention. The structure was simplified: secondary metrics moved into expandable views, while the main screen kept information directly related to everyday decisions. The savings experience was also reframed around goal progress rather than transaction history."],["Final Product & Deliverables","The final mobile product brings together daily expense management, budget planning, savings goals and financial analytics. Its design connects short-term actions with longer-term goals without requiring accounting knowledge."],["Reflection & Competencies","The defining task was deciding which information deserved attention, not drawing financial charts. Monday Balance demonstrates mobile UX, financial data visualisation, information architecture, progressive disclosure, accessible UI and product thinking."]]},"vetra":{"name":"Willow Practice","industry":"Veterinary Healthcare / B2B SaaS","platform":"Responsive web application","location":"Utrecht, Netherlands","role":"UX/UI Designer & Product Designer","scope":"Product discovery · Workflow analysis · Information architecture · UX/UI design · Prototyping","strapline":"Designing a more efficient veterinary workflow","sections":[["Project Overview","<p>Willow Practice is a veterinary practice management platform designed to support the daily operations of small veterinary clinics.</p><p>The product connects appointment scheduling, animal records, consultation histories, staff workflows and administrative responsibilities within a unified responsive web application.</p><p>The project addressed a common challenge in professional healthcare environments: essential information is often available, but not necessarily accessible in the right context or at the right moment.</p><p>Veterinary clinics manage relationships between animal patients, their owners, appointments, medical histories, treatments and follow-up care.</p><p>These relationships make veterinary software fundamentally different from a conventional customer-management system.</p><p>A receptionist arranging an appointment needs fast access to availability and client information. A veterinarian conducting a consultation needs immediate visibility into the animal's medical history. A clinic administrator needs a broader understanding of scheduling, staff responsibilities and operational activity.</p><p>Although these users interact with the same underlying information, their priorities differ significantly.</p><p>My responsibility was to design a coherent product experience that could accommodate these differences without fragmenting the system.</p><p>The work included workflow analysis, information architecture, role-sensitive navigation, patient profiles, appointment scheduling interfaces, structured consultation records, administrative dashboards and follow-up workflows.</p><p>The objective was to reduce unnecessary administrative complexity while maintaining access to information essential for professional decision-making.</p><p>Willow Practice demonstrates a product design approach centered on operational reliability, contextual information and the practical needs of professional users.</p>"],["Business Context","<h3>The complexity behind a veterinary appointment</h3><p>A veterinary clinic operates through multiple interconnected activities.</p><p>An appointment may begin with a phone conversation or registration request, but the complete service journey includes scheduling, identifying the patient, reviewing relevant medical history, conducting an examination, documenting treatment and planning further care.</p><p>Each stage generates information that may be needed by another member of the clinic.</p><p>The receptionist needs scheduling information.</p><p>The veterinarian needs medical context.</p><p>The administrator needs operational visibility.</p><p>When these processes are handled through separate tools, even routine tasks become unnecessarily complicated.</p><p>Small veterinary practices may depend on a combination of paper records, spreadsheets, messaging applications and appointment calendars.</p><p>Although each tool can support an individual activity, disconnected systems create problems when information needs to move between employees and different stages of care.</p><h3>The product opportunity</h3><p>Willow Practice was designed to centralize these relationships within one structured environment.</p><p>Rather than simply replacing paper forms with digital equivalents, the project focused on how information could support the complete clinic workflow.</p><p>The product needed to accommodate both repetitive administrative actions and information-intensive veterinary consultations.</p><p>This created a central design challenge:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*How can a veterinary management platform provide comprehensive clinical information without overwhelming the people who need to use it every day?*</em></p>"],["Problem Definition","<p>The initial product requirements identified several recurring operational difficulties.</p><h3>01. Fragmented patient information</h3><p>Animal records, appointment details and treatment histories could become difficult to navigate when maintained across different systems.</p><h3>02. Scheduling complexity</h3><p>Receptionists needed to coordinate appointments while accounting for veterinarian availability and existing bookings.</p><h3>03. Time-consuming medical record retrieval</h3><p>Veterinarians required access to previous consultation information without repeatedly navigating through unrelated records.</p><h3>04. Manual follow-up coordination</h3><p>Vaccinations and planned return visits required a clearer structure for tracking upcoming responsibilities.</p><h3>05. Limited operational visibility</h3><p>Clinic administrators needed a consolidated overview of daily activity and staff responsibilities.</p><h3>06. Competing information priorities</h3><p>Administrative information and medical information were both important, but they were not equally relevant during every task.</p><p>These challenges demonstrated that the product could not be designed as a collection of independent screens.</p><p>It needed an information architecture that reflected the relationships between patients, appointments, employees and treatment records.</p>"],["Users & Professional Responsibilities","<p>The experience was designed around three primary professional roles.</p><h3>Receptionist</h3><p><strong>Primary responsibility:</strong> Coordinating appointments and managing the administrative entry points of the patient journey.</p><p>The receptionist needs to register patients, associate them with their owners, create appointments, review veterinarian availability and manage scheduling information.</p><p>These tasks are performed frequently and require fast, predictable interactions.</p><p>The interface therefore needed to minimize unnecessary data entry and support scheduling without constant navigation between unrelated screens.</p><h3>Veterinarian</h3><p><strong>Primary responsibility:</strong> Reviewing patient information and documenting clinical care.</p><p>The veterinarian needs access to relevant medical history, previous consultations, recorded findings, diagnoses and treatment recommendations.</p><p>During a consultation, the priority shifts from general administrative information to clinical context.</p><p>The interface needed to present medical records clearly while retaining access to owner and appointment details when necessary.</p><h3>Clinic Administrator</h3><p><strong>Primary responsibility:</strong> Maintaining visibility into the clinic's operations.</p><p>The administrator requires access to staff schedules, service configuration, operational information, reporting and permission-related settings.</p><p>This role needs a broader perspective than individual appointments or patient consultations.</p><h3>Shared information, different priorities</h3><p>All three roles interact with the same patient and appointment records.</p><p>The challenge was to create differentiated interfaces without producing isolated information systems.</p><p>This principle became fundamental to the product architecture.</p>"],["Discovery & Workflow Analysis","<h3>Understanding the complete service journey</h3><p>The design process began with mapping a typical veterinary appointment from initial registration to follow-up planning.</p><p>The workflow included:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Registration → Scheduling → Arrival → Consultation → Treatment Documentation → Payment Recording → Follow-Up Planning*</em></p><p>This sequence revealed how information created in one stage becomes important during another.</p><p>A patient registered by reception later appears in the veterinarian's appointment schedule.</p><p>The veterinarian uses the existing record to understand previous medical activity.</p><p>Information recorded during a consultation becomes part of the patient's ongoing history.</p><p>Follow-up actions may create additional scheduling requirements.</p><p>The design needed to preserve continuity throughout this process.</p><h3>Identifying task-specific requirements</h3><p>Two particularly important requirements emerged.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Administrative tasks needed to be fast.*</em></p><p>Repeated scheduling and registration actions should not require unnecessarily complex forms.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Clinical tasks needed sufficient information.*</em></p><p>Veterinarians should not have to sacrifice access to medical context in exchange for a visually simpler interface.</p><h3>Design implication</h3><p>These requirements suggested that the product should adapt information priority to the task being performed.</p><p>A single screen structure would not be equally effective for every role.</p><p>The solution therefore required role-sensitive interface patterns supported by shared records.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>Research methodology note: The source documentation establishes workflow mapping and role-based requirements analysis. It does not establish formal clinical field studies, controlled usability tests or a quantified participant sample.</em></p>"],["UX Strategy","<p>The UX strategy focused on three connected principles.</p><h3>01. Organize around professional workflows</h3><p>Navigation and interface actions needed to reflect how clinic employees perform their responsibilities.</p><p>The objective was to avoid forcing users to understand the product's internal structure before completing ordinary tasks.</p><h3>02. Maintain information continuity</h3><p>Patient profiles, appointments and consultation records needed to remain meaningfully connected.</p><p>Users should be able to move between related information without repeatedly searching for the same record.</p><h3>03. Prioritize context over visual uniformity</h3><p>Different professional tasks require different information hierarchies.</p><p>During appointment scheduling, availability and timing are primary.</p><p>During a consultation, medical history becomes more important.</p><p>During administrative review, operational status and staff information take priority.</p><p>The product therefore needed a consistent design system capable of supporting different contextual arrangements.</p><p>The aim was not to make every screen look identical.</p><p>It was to make the system behave predictably while presenting information according to its relevance.</p>"],["Information Architecture","<p>Willow Practice was organized around six primary modules.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Primary Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dashboard</td><td>Daily clinic activity and upcoming appointments</td></tr><tr><td>Calendar</td><td>Scheduling, appointments and staff availability</td></tr><tr><td>Patients</td><td>Animal profiles and medical histories</td></tr><tr><td>Clients</td><td>Owner profiles and contact information</td></tr><tr><td>Treatments</td><td>Consultation records and follow-up actions</td></tr><tr><td>Administration</td><td>Staff, service settings and reporting</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Why this structure?</h3><p>The architecture distinguishes between major categories of work without separating information that needs to remain connected.</p><p>The Calendar supports scheduling decisions.</p><p>Patients provides the clinical record of each animal.</p><p>Clients maintains information about the people responsible for those animals.</p><p>Treatments organizes consultation-related information.</p><p>Administration supports broader operational responsibilities.</p><p>Dashboard provides an overview of relevant activity.</p><h3>The patient as the central information object</h3><p>A critical architectural decision was to make the animal patient record the central connection between different modules.</p><p>This allows related appointments, medical histories and follow-up information to be organized around one identifiable patient.</p><p>The structure reflects the reality that veterinary care is longitudinal.</p><p>An animal may return for multiple consultations, treatments and follow-up visits.</p><p>The system therefore needed to represent an ongoing relationship rather than a disconnected series of appointments.</p>"],["Unified Patient Profile","<h3>A central location for clinical context</h3><p>The patient profile was one of the most important interfaces in the application.</p><p>Each animal received a dedicated record containing identification information, owner details, medical history and upcoming appointments.</p><p>The objective was to provide a recognizable point of reference across the clinic workflow.</p><h3>Chronological medical history</h3><p>Related records were organized chronologically.</p><p>This allows veterinarians to understand previous consultations and locate relevant medical information within the patient's history.</p><p>Chronology is especially important when evaluating ongoing treatment or reviewing previous decisions.</p><h3>Connecting medical and administrative information</h3><p>An animal's owner and contact details remain relevant to the patient record.</p><p>However, the importance of this information varies by task.</p><p>During registration, owner information may be essential.</p><p>During consultation, previous clinical findings may require greater visual priority.</p><p>The patient profile needed to accommodate both circumstances.</p><h3>UX rationale</h3><p>A unified patient profile reduces the need to reconstruct information from separate records.</p><p>It also provides a consistent starting point for different professional roles.</p><p>Rather than treating the patient as a single database entry, the interface presents the patient as the connection between appointments, clinical history and future care.</p>"],["Appointment Scheduling & Calendar Design","<h3>Designing for frequent administrative interactions</h3><p>Appointment scheduling is one of the most repetitive activities in a veterinary clinic.</p><p>Receptionists need to review availability, register appointments, identify potential conflicts and access booking information quickly.</p><p>A scheduling interface that requires excessive navigation can slow down routine work.</p><h3>Split-view calendar</h3><p>I designed a split-view appointment interface combining a scheduling timeline with a contextual appointment panel.</p><p>This allows a receptionist to review appointment details without leaving the calendar.</p><p>The interaction pattern preserves visibility of the broader schedule while presenting information about an individual booking.</p><h3>Maintaining context</h3><p>The value of this arrangement lies in its ability to keep related information visible simultaneously.</p><p>A receptionist can consider an appointment in relation to other scheduled activity rather than opening an isolated detail page and losing sight of the timetable.</p><h3>Scheduling information</h3><p>The calendar supports the relationship between appointments and staff availability.</p><p>This is important because a booking cannot be evaluated solely as an available time slot.</p><p>It must also be understood in the context of the clinic's professional resources.</p><h3>UX rationale</h3><p>The split-view pattern reduces unnecessary transitions between scheduling and detail views.</p><p>Its purpose is to support a faster, more predictable appointment-management experience.</p>"],["Simplifying Appointment Creation","<h3>The problem with overly demanding forms</h3><p>An appointment-registration process may eventually require several pieces of information.</p><p>However, not every detail is necessarily available during the first interaction.</p><p>Requiring all fields immediately can make an otherwise simple scheduling task feel unnecessarily complicated.</p><h3>The initial approach</h3><p>The early appointment creation flow required more information during the first stage.</p><p>This increased the amount of work needed before an appointment could be registered.</p><h3>The revised approach</h3><p>The flow was simplified by reducing the number of required fields during initial scheduling.</p><p>Additional information could be completed later.</p><h3>UX rationale</h3><p>This decision separated the information necessary to schedule an appointment from details that could be collected at another stage.</p><p>The change supported the receptionist's primary task: securing the appropriate appointment without unnecessary interruption.</p><h3>Broader product principle</h3><p>A form should request information according to when that information is needed.</p><p>Completeness remains important, but it does not always need to be achieved in one interaction.</p>"],["Clinical Consultation Workflow","<h3>Designing for information-intensive tasks</h3><p>The consultation interface required a different approach from the scheduling experience.</p><p>A veterinarian may need to review previous medical history while documenting new information.</p><p>The design therefore needed to support both reading and recording clinical information.</p><h3>Structured consultation records</h3><p>Rather than relying exclusively on an unstructured note field, the consultation interface separates relevant information into recognizable categories.</p><p>These include:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Symptoms*</em></p><p>Information describing the patient's presenting condition.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Examination Findings*</em></p><p>Observations recorded during the clinical examination.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Diagnosis*</em></p><p>The clinician's documented assessment.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Treatment Recommendations*</em></p><p>Information about recommended care and subsequent actions.</p><h3>Why structure matters</h3><p>Structured sections make clinical records easier to scan and interpret.</p><p>They also help distinguish different types of information within the same consultation.</p><p>This does not eliminate the need for professional judgment.</p><p>The interface supports documentation and retrieval rather than attempting to make medical decisions.</p><h3>UX rationale</h3><p>The goal was to improve the organization of information without forcing medical documentation into a confusing administrative layout.</p><p>The design prioritizes clarity while preserving the clinical context required by the veterinarian.</p>"],["Medical Information Hierarchy","<h3>Not all information is equally important at the same moment</h3><p>An early version of the patient profile gave owner information and clinical records similar visual emphasis.</p><p>Although both types of information were relevant, this arrangement created unnecessary competition for attention.</p><p>During a consultation, a veterinarian's immediate concern is usually the animal's medical information rather than the complete administrative profile of the owner.</p><h3>The revised hierarchy</h3><p>The patient profile was reorganized to give medical history greater prominence during clinical tasks.</p><p>Owner information remained accessible, but no longer competed equally with the most important clinical content.</p><h3>The design decision</h3><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Administrative and medical records had equal visual priority.</p><p><strong>Decision:</strong> Foreground medical history in the consultation context.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Support task-specific information retrieval while preserving access to related administrative details.</p><h3>A broader UX lesson</h3><p>Consistency does not mean every information category must receive the same visual treatment.</p><p>The hierarchy should reflect the user's current responsibility.</p><p>This principle influenced the relationship between patient information, appointments and consultation records throughout the product.</p>"],["Role-Based Navigation","<h3>One product, different working environments</h3><p>The application serves employees with different professional responsibilities.</p><p>A receptionist does not need the same set of primary actions as a veterinarian or clinic administrator.</p><p>Displaying every available function with equal prominence would create unnecessary visual noise.</p><h3>Role-sensitive interfaces</h3><p>The interface was designed to adapt its primary actions according to the user's responsibilities.</p><p>The receptionist experience emphasizes scheduling and registration.</p><p>The veterinarian experience emphasizes patient history and consultation documentation.</p><p>The administrative experience provides broader operational tools.</p><h3>Shared records</h3><p>Although the interface priorities differ, users continue to interact with related patient and appointment information.</p><p>The goal was to create specialized working experiences within a coherent product structure.</p><h3>UX rationale</h3><p>Role-based navigation reduces the effort required to find frequently used functions.</p><p>It also limits unnecessary exposure to actions that are irrelevant to the user's work.</p><p>The documented design addresses role-sensitive navigation. It should not be presented as proof of a separately implemented security or permissions backend without additional technical evidence.</p>"],["Dashboard & Operational Overview","<h3>From individual tasks to clinic-wide visibility</h3><p>The Dashboard provides an overview of daily clinic activity and upcoming appointments.</p><p>Unlike the patient profile, which focuses on a specific animal, the dashboard is concerned with the wider operational context.</p><p>The primary challenge was information density.</p><p>Clinic employees need an overview without losing the distinction between information requiring immediate attention and information that is merely informative.</p><h3>Information priorities</h3><p>The dashboard was organized around daily activity and scheduling awareness.</p><p>Its purpose was to support navigation toward relevant operational work rather than reproduce every record in one screen.</p><h3>Professional information design</h3><p>The dashboard needed clear labels, recognizable states and a consistent layout.</p><p>These principles are especially important in environments where users return to the same interfaces throughout the working day.</p><h3>UX rationale</h3><p>An effective operational dashboard should make the next relevant action easier to identify.</p><p>Its value is not determined by the number of metrics displayed.</p><p>It is determined by how clearly those metrics and records support daily decisions.</p>"],["Client & Owner Information","<p>Veterinary care involves two related but distinct identities: the animal receiving treatment and the person responsible for that animal.</p><p>The product needed to represent both without confusing their roles.</p><h3>Animal patient</h3><p>The patient record organizes clinical information, identification details and treatment history.</p><h3>Animal owner</h3><p>The client record maintains owner-related information and contact details.</p><h3>Relationship between records</h3><p>The interface connects these two categories while preserving their different purposes.</p><p>This distinction is important because clinical documentation belongs to the animal's history, while communication and certain administrative responsibilities relate to the owner.</p><h3>UX rationale</h3><p>Separating patient and client information at the architectural level supports clearer records.</p><p>Connecting them through the interface allows relevant details to remain accessible without creating unnecessary duplication or confusion.</p>"],["Follow-Up Care & Reminders","<h3>Extending the journey beyond a single appointment</h3><p>Veterinary care often requires ongoing attention.</p><p>An initial consultation may result in a planned return visit, while vaccination schedules and follow-up responsibilities need to remain visible over time.</p><p>The product therefore included a dedicated follow-up workflow.</p><h3>Organizing upcoming responsibilities</h3><p>Upcoming vaccinations and planned return visits were structured as follow-up items.</p><p>The objective was to make these requirements easier to identify and manage within the broader patient journey.</p><h3>Connecting historical and future information</h3><p>Medical history helps explain what has already happened.</p><p>Follow-up information helps indicate what needs attention next.</p><p>Both are relevant to the patient's continuing care.</p><h3>UX rationale</h3><p>A well-organized veterinary record should support more than reviewing previous events.</p><p>It should also help professionals understand the next planned stage of care.</p><p>The documentation confirms the design of follow-up workflows. It does not establish integration with an external SMS, email or push-notification provider, so such functionality is not claimed.</p>"],["Visual Design & UI System","<h3>Calmness as a functional requirement</h3><p>Willow Practice was designed for a professional environment in which information clarity matters more than decorative novelty.</p><p>The visual direction used neutral backgrounds, restrained teal accents, consistent status indicators and readable typography.</p><p>The objective was to establish a calm interface suitable for repeated daily use.</p><h3>Typography</h3><p>Clear typographic hierarchy helps employees distinguish between patient identities, appointment information, medical records and supporting administrative details.</p><h3>Information density</h3><p>Professional software frequently needs to display substantial amounts of information.</p><p>The goal was not to remove useful details simply to create a minimalist appearance.</p><p>It was to organize those details so that users could identify relevant content efficiently.</p><h3>Status communication</h3><p>Consistent visual states help communicate appointment conditions, warnings and incomplete information.</p><p>These distinctions support predictable interaction across multiple areas of the application.</p><h3>Medical versus administrative content</h3><p>The visual system maintains a recognizable distinction between clinical information and administrative records.</p><p>This helps preserve context when users move between different tasks.</p><h3>Design principle</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Professional interfaces should make complex information easier to use without hiding the complexity that professionals genuinely need.*</em></p>"],["Responsive Design & Consultation Context","<p>The product was designed as a responsive web application.</p><p>This allowed the interface to accommodate different device sizes and working contexts.</p><h3>Desktop workflows</h3><p>Larger screens provide space for scheduling timelines, dashboards and more information-dense administrative views.</p><h3>Tablet considerations</h3><p>The design also accounted for tablet use within consultation rooms.</p><p>In this context, a veterinarian may need to review patient information without relying on a conventional desk-based workflow.</p><h3>Responsive information hierarchy</h3><p>The interface needed to preserve essential content while adapting the way that content was arranged.</p><p>The challenge was to maintain readability and recognizable interactions across different screen sizes.</p><h3>Contextual usability</h3><p>A responsive clinical interface must remain useful in the circumstances in which professionals work.</p><p>This requirement influenced the approach to patient histories, consultation records and visual clarity.</p><p>The project documentation establishes responsive design and tablet considerations. It does not claim a native mobile application or specific medical-device integrations.</p>"],["Interface States & Error Awareness","<p>Professional workflows cannot be designed exclusively around ideal conditions.</p><p>The interface also considered situations where information was incomplete or scheduling conditions required attention.</p><h3>Appointment conflicts</h3><p>Scheduling information may contain conflicting conditions that need to be recognizable within the interface.</p><h3>Incomplete records</h3><p>A patient or consultation record may not yet contain all relevant information.</p><p>The design needed clear ways to communicate incomplete states.</p><h3>Warnings</h3><p>Important conditions require consistent visual presentation so they can be distinguished from ordinary supporting information.</p><h3>Readable patient histories</h3><p>Chronological medical information needs a consistent structure that remains understandable as more records are added.</p><h3>UX rationale</h3><p>Clear states reduce ambiguity.</p><p>In professional environments, ambiguity can create additional work because employees must stop and determine whether information is complete, current or relevant.</p><p>The objective was to make the interface's meaning as predictable as possible.</p><p>This is an interface-design consideration, not a claim of clinically certified error prevention.</p>"],["Prototyping & Design Iteration","<p>The source documentation identifies two significant refinements during the design process.</p><h3>Iteration 01. Reprioritizing patient information</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Initial approach*</em></p><p>The original patient profile presented owner information and clinical records with similar visual emphasis.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Both categories competed for attention, even when the veterinarian's immediate task required medical context.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Revised approach*</em></p><p>The layout was reorganized to foreground medical history during consultation-related work.</p><p>Owner information remained accessible but became less visually dominant.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX rationale*</em></p><p>The interface should prioritize information according to the user's current professional task.</p><h3>Iteration 02. Simplifying appointment creation</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Initial approach*</em></p><p>The appointment flow required more information during the initial scheduling interaction.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>The form introduced unnecessary work before the receptionist could complete the primary action.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Revised approach*</em></p><p>The number of initially required fields was reduced.</p><p>Additional information could be completed at a later stage.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX rationale*</em></p><p>Separating immediate requirements from supplementary information makes repetitive tasks more efficient and predictable.</p><h3>What these iterations revealed</h3><p>Both revisions addressed the same broader design problem: unnecessary complexity created by presenting information at the wrong moment.</p><p>The first iteration reduced visual competition.</p><p>The second reduced interaction friction.</p><p>Together, they reinforced the importance of contextual information architecture.</p>"],["Final Product, Deliverables & Design Outcomes","<p>Willow Practice brings together several areas of veterinary practice management within one coherent interface.</p><h3>Patient management</h3><p>Dedicated animal profiles connect identification information, owner details, medical histories and upcoming appointments.</p><h3>Appointment scheduling</h3><p>A split-view calendar supports scheduling and the review of appointment details without unnecessary context switching.</p><h3>Clinical documentation</h3><p>Structured consultation records organize symptoms, examination findings, diagnoses and treatment recommendations.</p><h3>Role-sensitive workflows</h3><p>Primary navigation and actions are adapted to the responsibilities of receptionists, veterinarians and administrators.</p><h3>Follow-up coordination</h3><p>A dedicated workflow organizes planned return visits and vaccination-related responsibilities.</p><h3>Administrative visibility</h3><p>The platform includes dashboard and administrative areas supporting clinic activity, staff-related information, service settings and reporting.</p><h3>My design deliverables</h3><p>My involvement covered:</p><ul><li>Workflow analysis and requirements interpretation.</li><li>UX strategy.</li><li>Information architecture.</li><li>Role-based interface planning.</li><li>Patient profile design.</li><li>Appointment scheduling flows.</li><li>Consultation record structure.</li><li>Dashboard design.</li><li>Follow-up workflow design.</li><li>Responsive UI.</li><li>Visual hierarchy and interface states.</li><li>Prototyping and design iteration.</li></ul><h3>Evaluating the results</h3><p>The documented final design provides a more organized relationship between patient records, appointments and professional workflows.</p><p>The intended benefits include clearer information retrieval, reduced unnecessary administrative steps and more predictable navigation.</p><p>However, the project documentation does not provide measured clinical efficiency improvements, verified staff time savings, usability-test statistics or evidence of deployment within an operating clinic.</p><p>These design outcomes should therefore be understood as the capabilities and objectives of the developed product experience, not independently measured business results.</p>"],["My Contribution & Reflection","<p>Willow Practice required a different approach from consumer-facing digital products.</p><p>The defining challenge was not creating a visually distinctive interface.</p><p>It was designing a system in which multiple professional roles could work with complex, interconnected information.</p><p>The project reinforced the importance of understanding the structure of a real workflow before designing individual screens.</p><p>An appointment is connected to a patient.</p><p>A patient is connected to an owner.</p><p>A consultation is connected to medical history.</p><p>A follow-up is connected to previous care and future scheduling.</p><p>These relationships determine how the product needs to be organized.</p><p>My role involved translating that operational complexity into a coherent UX architecture.</p><p>I worked on the structure of the application, its professional user flows, the relationships between data and screens, and the visual and interaction patterns required to make the product usable.</p><p>The most important decisions concerned the hierarchy of information.</p><p>Medical history needed greater prominence during consultations.</p><p>Receptionists needed fewer unnecessary steps when creating appointments.</p><p>Different professional roles needed different primary actions without losing access to shared records.</p><p>These decisions illustrate how UX design can support professional work through clarity, context and carefully organized complexity.</p><p>Willow Practice demonstrates my experience in:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*B2B SaaS Product Design*</em></p><p>Creating structured interfaces for professional operational environments.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Healthcare UX*</em></p><p>Designing information architecture around clinical workflows and patient histories.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Role-Based Interface Design*</em></p><p>Adapting navigation and primary actions to different professional responsibilities.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Complex Dashboard Design*</em></p><p>Organizing information-dense screens for clarity and operational relevance.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Connecting patients, clients, appointments, consultations and follow-up records.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Interaction Design*</em></p><p>Reducing unnecessary navigation and simplifying repetitive administrative tasks.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Responsive Product Design*</em></p><p>Considering different working environments, including desktop administration and tablet-based consultations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Product Thinking*</em></p><p>Evaluating interface decisions in relation to the complete service workflow rather than isolated screens.</p><p>The project illustrates a principle that continues to guide my approach to professional software design:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*The goal is not to make complex work appear simple. It is to make complex work easier to understand and perform.*</em></p>"]]}};
if (typeof module !== "undefined") module.exports = { productCases };
